WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
Imagine choosing what you need, getting all the way to checkout, and discovering that the last button

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.921
is out of reach. You should not have to ask somebody else to finish. Can I Finish

3
00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:17.180
helps developers investigate that barrier with a concrete keyboard task and evidence they can rerun.

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:23.819
This is our fictional Field Notes storefront. Everything uses PayPal's sandbox, so no real money or shipment

5
00:00:23.819 --> 00:00:30.459
is involved. In the broken version, the checkout action is a div with a button role and

6
00:00:30.459 --> 00:00:37.098
a click handler. It never receives focus during eighteen Tab presses. The report keeps the exact target

7
00:00:37.098 --> 00:00:38.660
markup and focus sequence.

8
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:45.670
The experimental AI feature runs an open model on your device. It explains supplied runner findings and

9
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:52.681
relevant source code, rather than pretending to discover a defect independently. In a registration example, it proposes

10
00:00:52.681 --> 00:00:59.691
replacing the div with a native button. A model suggestion is not proof of a repair. The

11
00:00:59.691 --> 00:01:02.165
browser task must be run again.

12
00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:09.392
After that merchant control is corrected, the same keyboard contract reaches PayPal's real sandbox sign in screen

13
00:01:09.392 --> 00:01:16.618
in three key events. The runner uses keyboard events, not mouse clicks or direct focus calls. This

14
00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:23.845
contract deliberately stops at sign in. Buyer authentication and payment approval are separate steps, and we label

15
00:01:23.845 --> 00:01:25.120
that boundary explicitly.

16
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:32.276
Separately, we completed an actual sandbox purchase with the generated test buyer. The entrant pressed Pay, and

17
00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:39.431
the application captured the approved order. We then read the order back from PayPal's API. Both the

18
00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:46.587
order and capture were completed for one U.S. dollar in sandbox funds. This saved record contains shortened

19
00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:47.850
references, not credentials.

20
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:54.368
If a shopper cancels, the keyboard journey returns to the merchant with a clear retry path. We

21
00:01:54.368 --> 00:02:00.886
separately checked that the cancelled order had no capture. The server binds each order to a signed

22
00:02:00.886 --> 00:02:07.405
session, verifies the amount, and makes capture retries idempotent. A receipt message alone is never treated as

23
00:02:07.405 --> 00:02:08.555
proof of payment.

24
00:02:08.555 --> 00:02:15.884
Inspect a screenshot for every recorded key, compare the same task before and after the fix, then

25
00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:23.213
export an executable regression test. The hosted evidence viewer needs no paid browser service. New automated journeys

26
00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:30.542
use the local runner. The open model requires a capable browser and an initial download, and its

27
00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:31.835
suggestions remain experimental.

28
00:02:31.835 --> 00:02:38.728
This prototype does not certify accessibility, and it has not yet been validated in a user study.

29
00:02:38.728 --> 00:02:45.622
It offers something specific: a repeatable task, a visible barrier, a verified merchant fix, and a real

30
00:02:45.622 --> 00:02:52.515
sandbox integration. Before asking a customer to pay, make sure they can reach the last step independently.